Database → MongoDB → Vector DB → RAG --- Simple Class Notes
Style: These are practical notes from the class, written like a story so the flow is easy to remember.
Source: Uploaded class transcript.
1. Where the class starts
The previous class was mainly about SQL databases.
The idea was simple:
Application → API → Database
The application sends a request through an API, and the API talks to the database. We can then insert, read, update, or delete data.
This class says: SQL is not the only type of database we need.
As we start building real applications, especially AI applications, we will meet different kinds of databases.
The class focuses on two important ones:
MongoDB → NoSQL / document-based database
Vector Database → useful for AI, semantic search and RAG
The vector database used in the class is called Qdrant (the transcript sometimes says "Quadrant").
2. First stop: MongoDB
Imagine SQL like a neatly organized Excel sheet.
You have:
rows
columns
column names
data types
a defined schema
MongoDB works differently.
MongoDB is a document-based database.
Instead of thinking mainly in rows and columns, think:
"I have a document containing key-value information."
For example:
{
    "id": 1,
    "name": "Anju Kumar",
    "age": 30,
    "grade": "A",
    "email": "example@email.com",
    "skills": ["Python", "MongoDB"]
}
This looks very similar to a Python dictionary.
That is the important mental model.
3. MongoDB structure --- keep this picture in your head
Think about the hierarchy like this:
MongoDB
   ↓
Database
   ↓
Collection
   ↓
Documents
The class compares it with SQL:
SQL                         MongoDB
------------------------------------------------
Database                    Database
Table                       Collection
Row                         Document
Column/value                Key/value
The teacher specifically says collection is similar/equivalent to a table for understanding, but it is not exactly the same thing.
A MongoDB collection is flexible because documents do not have to look exactly identical.
4. Why MongoDB is flexible
This is the main reason the class is introducing MongoDB.
Suppose today we store:
id
name
age
grade
email
Tomorrow we decide that one document should also contain:
project
We can store it.
We don't have to think in the same rigid table-schema way as a traditional SQL table.
For example:
{
    "id": 2,
    "name": "Rahul",
    "age": 27,
    "project": "AI Chatbot"
}
Another document could contain different information.
So the big idea is:
MongoDB gives us flexibility in the structure of the data.
The transcript connects this idea with databases such as:
MongoDB
Cassandra
HBase
5. Connecting Python to MongoDB
The class already created the MongoDB connection earlier.
The basic flow is:
MongoDB Cluster
      ↓
Connection String
      ↓
Python / VS Code
      ↓
MongoDB Client
      ↓
