17. Why are we moving from MongoDB to Vector DB?
Now the class changes direction.
MongoDB solved one problem:
How can I store flexible documents?
But AI applications have another problem:
How can I search based on meaning?
This is where a Vector Database comes in.
18. What is a vector database?
Imagine I have this sentence:
"My name is Anshu Kumar. I used to teach in Super 30."
A normal database stores the text.
A vector database wants a mathematical representation of the meaning of that text.
So we do:
Text
 ↓
Embedding Model
 ↓
Numbers
 ↓
Vector
 ↓
Vector Database
The important word is:
Embedding
An embedding converts information such as text into a numerical representation.
19. Why convert text into numbers?
Computers ultimately work with numbers.
The class gives the basic idea:
English
Hindi
Telugu
Tamil
Images
Videos
Audio
All of these can eventually be represented mathematically so computers can perform calculations on them.
For our current topic:
Sentence
   ↓
Embedding Model
   ↓
Vector
The vector contains many numerical values.
20. Real-world problem: thousands of documents
Now imagine we work in:
HR
Finance
Legal
Sales
Each department may have thousands or hundreds of thousands of documents.
Suppose HR has:
Employee policy
Leave policy
Insurance policy
Salary policy
Performance policy
Benefits document
...
A traditional search system can search for keywords.
For example:
"leave policy"
It searches for those words.
But AI systems can try to understand the meaning behind a question.
That's where vector search becomes powerful.
21. The AI way of searching
Suppose the user asks:
"Who is Anshu Kumar?"
That is normal human language.
We can convert the question into a vector too.
User question
     ↓
Embedding Model
     ↓
Query Vector
Now we have:
Stored document vectors
          +
Query vector
          ↓
Similarity search
          ↓
Nearest / most relevant documents
This is the basic idea behind semantic search.
22. The important difference
Traditional keyword search:
Question
   ↓
Look for matching words
Vector search:
Question
   ↓
Convert question into vector
   ↓
Compare with stored vectors
   ↓
Find closest / most similar information
So vector search is about meaning/similarity, not just exact words.
This is one of the most important ideas from the class.
