51. Interview-friendly explanation
If an interviewer asks:
"What is a vector database?"
A simple answer:
A vector database stores numerical representations called embeddings and allows us to search for similar vectors. In GenAI applications, we can convert document chunks into embeddings, store them in a vector database, and later convert a user's question into an embedding to retrieve the most relevant documents. This is commonly used as the retrieval part of RAG.
"Why do we need embeddings?"
Embeddings convert text or other information into numerical vectors that capture semantic information. Because both documents and queries can be represented in the same vector space, we can compare them using similarity measures and retrieve relevant information.
"Why MongoDB instead of SQL?"
MongoDB is a document-based NoSQL database. It is useful when the data structure is flexible and documents may not all have exactly the same fields.
"What is Top-K?"
Top-K means retrieving the K most relevant results from the vector search. For example, Top-5 means return the five highest-ranked matching results.
52. Final mental model
If you remember only one story from this class, remember this:
First, we learned that not every application needs only SQL.
MongoDB gives us a flexible way to store documents.
Then we moved into the GenAI world.
AI applications often have huge amounts of documents, and normal keyword search is not enough for semantic questions.
So we take the documents, break them into smaller chunks, and use an embedding model to turn each chunk into numbers.
Those numbers are called vectors.
We store the vectors along with the original text/metadata in a vector database such as Qdrant.
When a user asks a question, we turn the question into a vector too.
Then the vector database finds the closest vectors.
Those closest results are our relevant information.
And this becomes the foundation for RAG, where the retrieved information is given to an LLM so it can produce a useful answer.
